     1	---
     2	name: dxb-team2
     3	description: Use when the CEO hands the construction a job — the default construction door. The lead (the session) chooses who writes — itself, a fork, medium helpers, or a fork with helpers — and verifies every piece; one blind, read-only GPT-6.1 Sol pass checks the work; its findings are fixed and tested; the CEO is warned to switch the effort — max for plan, design and architecture, high for the build. Trigger `/dxb-team2 <the job in one sentence>`.
     4	---
     5	
     6	# The team — one lead, the hands it chooses, one blind auditor
     7	
     8	The construction's default door <!-- CEO-OK: dxb-team2-default-door-2026-09-28 -->, and since
     9	2026-10-06 a lean one: no score card, no done-list, no Fable, no paperwork per job — the work, a blind
    10	check, the fixes, the tests <!-- CEO-OK: dxb-team2-lean-2026-10-06 -->. Every holding build runs
    11	through it, so the standard is the Ferrari's: fast because nothing in it is ceremony, right because
    12	nothing reaches him unchecked. The laws it obeys are in `.claude/CLAUDE.md`; it opens `dxb-verify`
    13	and `dxb-close-row`.
    14	
    15	## 1. The flow
    16	
    17	```
    18	THE CEO'S JOB, in one sentence he would sign
    19	   │
    20	   ├── small — a fix, a deletion, a text he already worded: no plan, straight to ARRANGE
    21	   │
    22	PLAN      the talk turns to plan, design or architecture → effort warning MAX (§2)
    23	          the plan goes to him once, in his language → his yes → effort warning HIGH (§2)
    24	   │
    25	ARRANGE   the lead chooses the hands (§3) and says it in one line
    26	   │
    27	BUILD     the hands write and run their own tests; the lead verifies every piece
    28	   │
    29	SOL       one blind, read-only pass (§4) — A blocks · B fixed now · C stays in the report
    30	   │
    31	FIX       a fork or a writing helper fixes; the lead verifies; no second Sol round
    32	   │
    33	TEST      the touched tests; code changed → the battery once (§5)
    34	   │
    35	COMMIT → TELL him in a few lines (§6) → STATE once, at the session's end
    36	```
    37	
    38	**What reaches him as a question:** starting a job, its plan (once), money out, a contract, an identity
    39	step, the Islamic boundaries, and an order of his that truly reads two ways — each with a
    40	recommendation, answerable in one word. Technical choices inside a job he said yes to are the lead's.
    41	The plan's single trip is his word <!-- CEO-OK: plan-comes-to-him-once-2026-10-03 -->.
    42	
    43	**The plan**, when there is one, names the scope, what it touches, the ordered steps, the
    44	arrangement, how it is verified, and the risks — in his language, short. No file changes before his yes.
    45	
    46	## 2. Effort — the session warns, he switches
    47	
    48	The session runs at `high`: the talk, the build, the fixes, the tests. Plan, design and architecture run
    49	at `max`. **He switches with /effort; the lead warns him — on every reply, until he has.**
    50	
    51	- The moment the talk turns to a plan, a design or an architecture, the lead runs
    52	  `python3 .claude/hooks/dxb-effort-warn.py plan` and opens that reply with
    53	  `⚠ Muhittin Bey, plan konuşmasındayız — /effort max'a geçin.`
    54	- At his yes to the plan, the lead runs `python3 .claude/hooks/dxb-effort-warn.py build`, opens the reply
    55	  with `⚠ Muhittin Bey, plan bitti — koda geçmeden /effort high'a geçin.`, and writes nothing until he
    56	  has switched. At the job's end: `python3 .claude/hooks/dxb-effort-warn.py off`.
    57	- Between those, the hook `dxb-effort-warn.py` repeats the line on each of his messages until the
    58	  session's live level matches — the lead cannot forget it. A small job has no plan and no warning.
    59	- Measured 2026-10-06: a hook's input carries no effort and /effort writes no transcript row, so the
    60	  live level is the newer, by timestamp, of the status line's record
    61	  (`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/claude-ctx/<session>.json`, field `effort`, which follows /effort at once) and
    62	  this session's last step in the transcript; neither known → the warning names an unknown level.
    63	  A skill's `effort` can raise a turn but never lower one — the reason the old design-at-max skill went.
    64	
    65	## 3. The seats and the arrangement
    66	
    67	| Seat | Who | Does | Never |
    68	|---|---|---|---|
    69	| **Lead** | the session, `high` | chooses the hands, writes what it keeps, verifies every piece and every fix, runs the tests, commits, tells him | approves its own work; commits a piece it has not verified; asks him a technical question |
    70	| **Fork** | the lead's own copy (`subagent_type: "fork"`), its whole context | writes a large piece that needs this conversation | commits |
    71	| **Writing helper** | `helper-writer` (Opus 5.5 · `medium`) | writes and tests a piece the lead specified; fixes a Sol finding | commits; widens the piece <!-- CEO-OK: helpers-write-code-under-lead-verification-2026-10-03 --> |
    72	| **Helper** | `helper` (Opus 5.5 · `medium`, read-only) | reads code or documents, measures, researches | writes |
    73	| **Reader** | a one-shot `scout` or `Explore` | a wide search whose text would swell the lead's context | writes; is resumed (its cache dies at 5 min) |
    74	| **Escalation writer** | `builder` (Opus 5.5 · `max`), one-shot | only a piece that failed twice at `high` | is opened for a routine fix |
    75	| **Auditor** | GPT-6.1 Sol <!-- CEO-OK: auditor-sol-6-1-2026-10-01 -->, through `scripts/governance/refuter.sh` | one blind pass per job (§4) | writes; audits a job twice; sees the lead's reasoning or verdict |
    76	
    77	Fable 5.1 is not consulted in a dxb-team2 job — neither the `advisor` tool (its own "before the work
    78	and before done" advice does not apply here) nor the Fable-based `design-eye`.
    79	
    80	**The arrangement — chosen at ARRANGE, said to him in one line with its why**
    81	<!-- CEO-OK: orchestration-three-arrangements-lead-chooses-2026-10-03 -->:
    82	
    83	| Arrangement | Who writes | When |
    84	|---|---|---|
    85	| **Lead** | the lead itself | a small job, or pieces so coupled that splitting them costs more than it saves |
    86	| **Fork** | the fork | one large piece that needs the whole conversation, while the lead stays free to verify |
    87	| **Team** | `helper-writer`s, in parallel; `helper`s read and research beside them | independent pieces, each specifiable in a paragraph |
    88	| **Hybrid** | the fork writes the core; `helper-writer`s the side pieces; `helper`s read | a large core with independent pieces around it |
    89	
    90	- Parallel writers never touch the same file. Each gets a spec it can work from alone: the files, the
    91	  behaviour, the measured facts it must not re-litigate, the test it writes first, what it reports.
    92	- The lead verifies each piece before it is committed — reads the diff, runs its tests — and commits
    93	  only what it verified. Nobody else commits.
    94	- A fork starts cheap: its first call reads the lead's cache (203,676 tokens read, 1,200 written —
    95	  session 5ed74ad7, 2026-10-03).
    96	- Code is written at `high`. Opus `xhigh` is never used for code (FrontierCode 51.4 %, the bottom of the
    97	  curve); `max` writes code only as the escalation writer — a medium try plus a high retry (USD 1.89)
    98	  is under a third of one `max` run (USD 6.19) and passes the same auditor. Prices in this door are
    99	  written USD, never with a dollar sign: opened with a job sentence, a dollar sign and a digit are
   100	  replaced by the sentence's words (measured 2026-10-04).
   101	
   102	## 4. Sol — one blind pass
   103	
   104	Every job that changes code, a rule or a door gets one Sol pass, after BUILD; a change to the records
   105	alone (STATE, the board) is checked by the rulers on commit instead.
   106	
   107	- **Launch:** `scripts/governance/refuter.sh [--effort medium|high|xhigh] "<brief>"` — `high` by
   108	  default; `xhigh` for money, a database, security, approval, governance or the agents; `medium` for a
   109	  text-only change. An audit can outlast the Bash tool's 10 minutes: launch it detached (a small
   110	  script under `setsid nohup`, its output to the job folder, `EXIT=` on its last line) and wait for
   111	  that line.
   112	- **The brief — blind, not in the dark:** his words verbatim; the plan's decisions as facts ("this door
   113	  is closed on purpose"); the range; where the raw test output lies. Never the lead's reasoning, a
   114	  summary, "tests are green", or another auditor's verdict. Its first question: *does this do what he
   115	  asked?*
   116	- **Its hand:** Sol queries the construction engine itself through its one tool `sql_read`
   117	  (`scripts/governance/sol-db-mcp.mjs`, role `sol_reader`) — the lead never hands over a count Sol can
   118	  take. Its sandbox has no network, so the lead runs the database-writing tests and hands their output
   119	  over whole.
   120	- **Its findings**, per claim: Claim · Verdict (REFUTED | STANDS AFTER ATTEMPTED REFUTATION |
   121	  UNVERIFIED) · Finding (`file:line`, scenario, impact) · Evidence · Correction required.
   122	  **A** blocks · **B** is fixed in this pass · **C** is older than this work and stays in the job's
   123	  report — never the board; a row opens only on his word.
   124	- **Fix:** a fork or a `helper-writer` fixes each A and B; the lead verifies the finding's own case.
   125	  There is no second Sol round <!-- CEO-OK: sol-single-pass-fixes-by-helper-2026-10-03 -->.
   126	- **Dispute:** the lead runs the evidence Sol gave — it fails → real, fixed; it holds → the finding drops
   127	  and the report says why. A question no test can settle is the lead's, with its reason in the report.
   128	- **Sol unavailable** (quota, error, timeout): on an ordinary job the `refuter` subagent (Opus 5.5 ·
   129	  `high`) audits once, and the report says *"audited by Opus instead of Sol — read-only by word, not by
   130	  tool — because …"*; on money, a database or security the lead waits for Sol's window and works on
   131	  something else. No job closes unaudited; the auditor is never swapped silently
   132	  <!-- CEO-OK: fallback-auditor-named-and-refuter-second-round-gone-2026-10-04 -->.
   133	
   134	## 5. Tests
   135	
   136	- New behaviour is proven by a test written first — red before the code, green after.
   137	- After each piece, its own tests. After Sol's fixes, the touched tests again.
   138	- **Code changed → the battery, once, at the end:** `pnpm construction:battery` — the whole suite in
   139	  both halves against the construction's own engine (port 54422), 1,486 tests, about 6 minutes
   140	  (2026-10-04). Launch it detached from a shell whose `oom_score_adj` is 100; green is the line
   141	  `BATTERY_GREEN`. It answers the one question no single test can: did this break anything else.
   142	- **Text only** → no battery. A door, the core or a record: the commit hook's rulers read those on
   143	  every commit. A persona's words: `bash scripts/persona-ruler.sh` (dxb-verify), which the commit
   144	  hook does not run.
   145	- The commit hook runs by itself on every commit: the secret scan (gitleaks); the records ruler when
   146	  STATE, the board or the ledger change; the opening ruler when the core, a skill or the opening hook
   147	  change; the research ruler and `tests/b46` when the research engine changes.
   148	
   149	## 6. Commit, tell, record
   150	
   151	- **Commit:** one per job, or one per independent piece; the message says what changed and why.
   152	- **Tell him** in his language, in a few lines, under three headings — *Sizi bekleyen · Değişen ·
   153	  Bulunan* <!-- CEO-OK: job-report-three-headings-2026-10-03 -->: what waits on him; what changed;
   154	  what was found outside the job, each with a recommendation. An empty heading is not written; a
   155	  defect fixed inside the job is not listed; the proof stays in the work until he asks.
   156	- **His words** are written once, in `scripts/governance/ceo-approvals.json`, and only where a record
   157	  must rest on his order or approval (LAW B) — never copied into commits, doors or STATE.
   158	- **STATE** is written once, at the session's end: the three headings, a few lines each — what
   159	  finished and waits for his eye, what is next, what waits on him. No conversation, no quotes, no
   160	  hashes or counts beyond the one that names a job.
   161	- **The job folder** (`.planning/quick/<date>-<slug>/`) holds Sol's report and the raw evidence — only
   162	  for a job that went to Sol.
   163	
   164	## 7. Long commands, context and handover
   165	
   166	- **The 4-minute rules** (held by `~/.claude/hooks/dxb-cost-gate.py`): a command over 4 minutes is the
   167	  lead's; a subagent idle over 4 minutes is never resumed — its cache is the 5-minute one.
   168	- **The context gate** (held by `~/.claude/hooks/dxb-context-gate.py`) and **the clean break**: from
   169	  50 % used, hand over at the first clean break — the piece finished, the tree committed, no battery
   170	  running, the report sent — even when reaching it takes the session to 55-58 %; never in the middle of
   171	  a piece <!-- CEO-OK: handover-at-the-clean-break-2026-10-06 -->. The gate refuses the Agent tool from
   172	  55 %, so past it the lead finishes only what its own hand can, and starts no new phase it cannot
   173	  finish by about 58 %.
   174	- **The handover** is done by the engineer, never the CEO
   175	  <!-- CEO-OK: handover-at-50-without-asking-2026-10-01 -->. A note in the scratchpad, in the author's
   176	  own voice: the job's place in the whole first, then his words of this job verbatim, every phase with
   177	  status and commit, the traps met, the first message the successor sends; session-only orders marked
   178	  as such; last, the path of this session's own conversation —
   179	  `~/.claude/projects/-home-dxb-DxB-Global-OS/$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID.jsonl` — with the line *if
   180	  anything here is unclear, read the part you need there*
