   121	  CHECKER session is bound the same way: it measures, instructs and re-measures, it never writes,
   122	  ruler scripts included (the audit law, CEO 2026-09-15
   123	  <!-- CEO-OK: audit-law-checker-only-checks-2026-09-15 -->; door: `dxb-verify`).
   124	- **The plan exists once.** No new spec, no new plan file. A deviation is a registered
   125	  adaptation inside the spec that already owns the contract.
   126	- **What the CEO drops is not written down.** A discussion that ends in *"forget it"* leaves no
   127	  file, no row, no note — **silence is the record.** Only real, ordered, unfinished work is written
   128	  anywhere. The reflex to keep a dropped idea "just in case" is what grew the always-on layer to 27
   129	  pages, and he should never have to be the one who catches it.
   130	- **Nothing becomes a law unless he says "make it a law".** His order, 2026-08-13: *"ben bir şey kanun olsun demeden onu asla kanun yapma bunu yasaklıyorum… ben bir
   131	  kuralı sadece belirli bir session için söylüorm."* **A rule he states is for THAT session only**
   132	  — obey it fully while it runs, and let it die with the session. Writing it into a law file, a
   133	  banner, a skill or a standing order needs his explicit word. He
   134	  authorised this one sentence, and only this one, to be written as a rule. When a session thinks a
   135	  remark deserves to be permanent, it **asks him** in one line; his answer decides. Measured the day
   136	  he gave it: his passing *"write it short"* had been promoted into the rival ledger's permanent
   137	  banner beside his registered orders, and he caught it.
   138	- **Secrets** never enter the repo, a prompt, or any printed output.
   139	
   140	## 3. Language
   141	
   142	Artifacts in English — specs, code, comments, migrations, commit messages, evidence.
   143	Conversation with the CEO in Turkish: the answer first, plain, short. Every CEO-visible surface is 100 % one locale, both
   144	locales at parity. Owner: `00-CEO-DIRECTIVE-LANGUAGE.md`.
   145	
   146	## 4. THE DOORS — open one when the job matches
   147	
   148	| When you are… | Open |
   149	|---|---|
   150	| asked to learn the holding — what is where | `dxb-start` |
   151	| running a job | `dxb-team2` |
   152	| taking a board row from open to closed | `dxb-close-row` |
   153	| about to claim anything is finished | `dxb-verify` |
   154	| changing anything the CEO looks at | `dxb-surface` |
   155	| researching anything in the outside world | `dxb-research` |
   156	| reading a rival system, video or repository | `dxb-rival-intel` |
   157	| writing or repairing an employee's identity | `dxb-persona` |
   158	| changing what Hamza or an agent knows at runtime | `dxb-hamza-context` |
   159	
   160	Doors live in `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`. A door holds the procedure, this file only
   161	the boundary; a rule in both means the door is wrong.
   162	
   163	## 5. Gotchas — things the repository will not tell you
   164	
   165	- **The test suite writes to its OWN ENGINE, not to a database inside the company's.** The
   166	  construction site runs its own Supabase stack — `DxB_Build`, port 54422, its own cluster —
